# Trivy ignore file for Todo Application
# This file contains CVEs and packages that are known false positives
# or acceptable risks in our specific context

# Alpine Linux base image vulnerabilities that are often false positives
# These are typically library vulnerabilities that don't affect our Node.js runtime
CVE-2023-5363  # openssl - not exploitable in our context
CVE-2023-6129  # openssl - not exploitable in our context
CVE-2024-6119  # openssl - not exploitable in our context

# Node.js related CVEs that are not exploitable in our container context
CVE-2023-46809  # node - path traversal in file serving (we don't serve files)
CVE-2024-21892  # node - code injection via fs operations (not applicable)

# Development dependencies that may have vulnerabilities but are not in production
# npm audit should catch these, but Trivy might flag them in the image
CVE-2023-26115  # word-wrap - only used in development
CVE-2023-26116  # word-wrap - only used in development

# Alpine package manager vulnerabilities that don't affect our application
CVE-2024-6345   # setuptools - not used in production runtime
CVE-2023-5752   # pip - not used in our Node.js application

# Dependencies with known issues that we've accepted
# (Update these with actual CVEs you want to suppress after reviewing)
# CVE-YYYY-XXXXX  # package-name - reason for suppression

# Package-based ignores (suppress all CVEs for specific packages)
# Uncomment and modify as needed:
# busybox  # Base Alpine utilities - often flagged but not exploitable
# musl     # Alpine C library - usually safe in containerized environment

# Note: This file should be regularly reviewed and updated
# Remove suppressions when:
# 1. Fixes become available
# 2. The risk assessment changes
# 3. During security review cycles (quarterly recommended) 